00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:59.780
find the corresponding y value. So I look
for two on the x axis and find the point on

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:06.520
the graph. If that has that x value, that's
right here. Now I can look at the y value

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:13.420
of that point looks like three, and therefore
g of two is equal to three. If I try to do

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:19.569
the same thing to evaluate g of five, I run
into trouble. Five is an x value, I look for

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:27.530
it on the x axis. But there's no point on
the graph that has that x value. Therefore,

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:34.300
g of five is undefined, or we can say it does
not exist. The question of what x values and

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:40.889
y values make sense for a function leads us
to a discussion of domain and range. The domain

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:46.449
of a function is all possible x values that
make sense for that function. The range is

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:51.830
the y values that makes sense for the function.
In this example, we saw that the x value of

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:58.319
five didn't have a corresponding y value for
this function. So the x value of five is not

00:05:58.319 --> 00:06:04.360
in the domain of our function J. To find the
